A||N||11||Predator||Advantages going to 17 inch wheels?||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||03-11-2001||07:39 PM||||I have the stock 14 inch wheels on my 1985 GT. I like them, but have kicked the idea around of putting 17 inch wheels on. I have read a lot on the forum about people putting wheels on for looks. Are there any real advantages to putting 17 inch wheels on? You still have the same clearance in the fendor wells, so for what you add in wheel you have to take away in tire. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||GoCartGuy||03-11-2001||11:38 PM||||In my opinion its mostly for looks.  I bought mine with 17" on it, but the previous owner also lowered it to 4 1/4" off the tar.  BTW, you will pay more for tires with the 17" bout $160 a piece.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000002||GoCartGuy||03-11-2001||11:40 PM||||Also forgot to mention, with the low profile tires you have to realize you can't tube them.  Any punctures gotta be plugged, cheap but not always reliable especially at the higher psi.  Mine rated at 42 psi.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||85seNUT||03-12-2001||12:13 AM||||iv got a disadvantage....harder to peel out in!! hehe||||reg||1
Z||000004||GoCartGuy||03-12-2001||12:30 AM||||85seNut,<BR>Who wants to peel anyways, chirp and boom.<BR>Besides, tires for 17's ain't cheap.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||03-12-2001||12:40 AM||||Handling.  Less sidewall = less flex.  A lowered Fiero with a rear swaybar, decent shocks/struts and 17" wheels will handle like it's on rails.  The biggest problem I've heard is that 17" wheels can get pretty heavy, and if you live in an area with alot of potholes you have to be careful not to dent the rim.||||reg||1
Z||000006||filthyscarecrow||03-12-2001||02:52 AM||||bigger rims also have the rotating mass farther from the axis of rotation. what you gain in handling, you can potentially lose in acceleration because the wheels act like they're heavier due to the (is it) higher rotational inertia (i don't remember from my physix classes...) <BR>sport compact car did a test on this once comparing 16-18" wheels and found that the test driver (some rally guy) liked the handling of the 18" wheels better (less squishiness in teh corner) but had faster lap times and straight line speeds with the 16" wheels (by almost 2mph average). not too much, adn probably not noticable on the street, but bigger wheels will cost in acceleration... 16" is as big as i would ever go. adn the other guys are right too, yuo'll pay out the arse for bigger tires. <P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||03-12-2001||07:59 AM||||But the look is awesome. If I seriously autoX or drag race then I would have my stock wheels or lighter with tires for that. Like you said, you won't notice difference on acceleration but looks?? Everyone would notice. The very first day I put my 17"s I stopped at a Wendys drive thru window and the cashier ask me; "How much does one of those cost?" I wasn't sure if answering "well about $120,000" or answering "you could find a good one for $4K"....||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||03-12-2001||08:03 AM||||If you live in pothole country- look out, a good pot hole can cut your tire and destroy your rim(not much sidewall for protection)||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||03-12-2001||08:29 AM||||Looks, a little everyday handling, but if your really pushing the car the stock 14s with and R compound tire will out perform the 17s. Mine have a treadwear of 160 which won't last long.. but at (shop price) $96 ea it's not a big deal if they wear out. but you'll still get plenty of mileage out of them... and I can power brake the car with the rest of the camaros and mustangs around here... the rotating mass can be an issue but once you get your air/fuel issues worked out you'll still be kicking everyones @$$ anyway.<P>I'm trying to sell my 17s.  I just saturday morning replaced both my back tires.. i had a flat (drove over a nail laiden board 5-6 punctures) asking $1300 have recipts for over $2k. I'm wanting 18s or 19s. those are only for looks the lack of sidewall make the ride terrible but i've got stockys for the track and 18s tend to score better at local shows.<P>toss on a set of Eibachs to lower the stance a bit but not slam the crap out of it. If your getting springs.. let me know i'll change them for ya... takes about 3 hours at the shop... I did mine -SE, GT, BlackGTs car, and another shop customers car (87 SC). it's just TOO easy! <P>glad you could make it Saturday!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Predator||03-12-2001||12:13 PM||||The wheels sound tempting Trav. If my wife wouldn't divorce me I would do it right now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I am still waiting on Kevin to send back a reply on the lean condition. I really like your wheels. Not knowing what it will cost on getting the lean condition fixed I had better hold off. It was nice seeing everbody Saturday. I am working on a t-shirt with our emblem. I will send you a *.jpg when I get it complete to see what you think. It is just a first try so I will probably modify it. Hanes T- shirt maker is what I am using on my computer, and it does a pretty good job. I will post the new Dyno results when I get the car fixed. Thanks guys for all the replies. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000011||1FST2M6||03-12-2001||12:43 PM||||if you have the logo in a .PSD or .eps file can you send it to me? keep us posted on your fuel quandry.. ||||reg||1
